<article class="env-lemma" id="0G9I">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0G9I">Lemma <span data-tag="0G9I">15.86.8</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a ring. Let $\alpha : K \to K'$ be a morphism of $D(R)$. Assume </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$H^ i(K) = H^ i(K') = 0$ for $i \not\in \{ -1, 0\} $ 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$H^0(\alpha )$ is an isomorphism and $H^{-1}(\alpha )$ is surjective. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> For any $f \in R$ if $f : K \to K$ is $0$, then $f : K' \to K'$ is $0$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Set $M = \mathop{\mathrm{Ker}}(H^{-1}(\alpha ))$. Then $\alpha $ fits into a distinguished triangle 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  M[1] \to K \to K' \to M[2]  \]
</div>
    <p>
       Since $K \to K' \xrightarrow {f} K'$ is zero by our assumption, we see that $f : K' \to K'$ factors over a map $M[2] \to K'$. However $\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits (M[2], K') = 0$ for example by Derived Categories, Lemma <a href="/tag/06XS" data-tag="06XS">13.27.3</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>